ng with her and of  course the realities of their situation  in Iraq are that there are moments of  high-stress periods of high stress mixed  with periods of boredom periods of  sitting around waiting for something to  happen and how they fill those hours I  think has a great deal to do with how  they feel when they return to the United  States after their deployments I there's  a in the old world war two Dell  paperbacks there was a message on the  back that said books are weapons and  when I first encountered that several  years ago I liked it I sort of made it  my mantra and and when cadets said you  know I can't take this to Iraq I said  well maybe you can not in the way you  think and I think that that kind of  terminology is it's sufficiently  combative for someone who teaches at a  military academy but I also think that  the value of literature of poetry in  particular poetry of genre I think that  can convey more truth several truths at  once is that it teaches cadets to  wrestle with ambiguity which is one of  the institution's priorities and I also  realize and I think the cadets realize  as well that they can take all the field  manuals they want but we know that this  conflict has many challenges for which  there are no answers there are no ready  solutions in field manuals and in the  regulations and that's when they're left  on their own and that's where I think a  rich imaginative life will help them in  very practical ways there's another  thing as well and that I believe poetry  literature but poetry in particular is  far more important to many of our allies  and our  than it ever has been to us and I think  that that's important in terms of  cultural awareness for them to recognize  I had a colleague friend of mine named  Al who was a lieutenant colonel and he  just retired from the Army but before he  retired he volunteered to deploy to  Afghanistan and he worked there with the  National Military Academy of Afghanistan  which actually models itsel