ng with her and of  course the realities of their situation  in Iraq are that there are moments of  high-stress periods of high stress mixed  with periods of boredom periods of  sitting around waiting for something to  happen and how they fill those hours I  think has a great deal to do with how  they feel when they return to the United  States after their deployments I there's  a in the old world war two Dell  paperbacks there was a message on the  back that said books are weapons and  when I first encountered that several  years ago I liked it I sort of made it  my mantra and and when cadets said you  know I can't take this to Iraq I said  well maybe you can not in the way you  think and I think that that kind of  terminology is it's sufficiently  combative for someone who teaches at a  military academy but I also think that  the value of literature of poetry in  particular poetry of genre I think that  can convey more truth several truths at  once is that it teaches cadets to  wrestle with ambiguity which is one of  the institution's priorities and I also  realize and I think the cadets realize  as well that they can take all the field  manuals they want but we know that this  conflict has many challenges for which  there are no answers there are no ready  solutions in field manuals and in the  regulations and that's when they're left  on their own and that's where I think a  rich imaginative life will help them in  very practical ways there's another  thing as well and that I believe poetry  literature but poetry in particular is  far more important to many of our allies  and our  than it ever has been to us and I think  that that's important in terms of  cultural awareness for them to recognize  I had a colleague friend of mine named  Al who was a lieutenant colonel and he  just retired from the Army but before he  retired he volunteered to deploy to  Afghanistan and he worked there with the  National Military Academy of Afghanistan  which actually models itself on West  Point and he had taught a few years  before the the poet Rumi in a class that  we gave to all the juniors at West Point  and he brought his little book of  Persian poets with him and he loved Rumi  and he kept reading him and he his  counterpart a colonel at the Military  Academy and Kabul  he brought the book with him to his  meetings and the colonel the Afghan  colonel was delighted that he brought  this book because he read Rumi as well  new Rumi by heart and al told me  afterwards he said you know I don't want  to say that this relationship would have  been a complete bust had I not brought  room a with me but it really helped and  it showed in particular he wrote to me  that he and the colonel were more alike  than alike and I think that it really  helped them as they were imagining their  curriculum and as they were imagining  how they might train military officers  for the Afghan army that this point of  connection was extremely helpful and so  in the answer the answer to the plebes  question I can't take that to Iraq was  actually for me sharing with him the  correspondence that I had had with a  former student and I wanted to share in  another passage a bit of that with you  it's a student named Andy I was actually  now in his third year of law school at  William and Mary he's the editor of the  Law Review there and after he graduates  he will become an army lawyer and his  motivation was his deployment he  actually had two deployments to Iraq and  he feels that our conflict there have  and the challenges it presents have  really outpaced our current notions of  the laws of land warfare and he thinks  he can make a difference by working as  an army lawyer his father was a general  so he knows how the army works but he  was also a bit of a rebel and that's why  I was quite fond of him in fact he knows  what it means to obey but he was most  remarkable in my class  so bang and I wanted to sort of  obedience and disobedience has obviously  been a both a professional and a  personal interest for a long time now  for me and so I just want to share a bit  of of what this particular students work  in the classroom meant to him as he was  deployed he was in a class on American  literature with man he was scheduled one  day to give a presentation on thorough  civil disobedience but he sent his paper  in with one of his classmates and he  didn't show up until the end of the hour  now elsewhere one would think fine you  don't show up for class no big deal but  at West Point things are different and  that should that's perhaps no surprise  if a cadets absent without an excuse or  even late he or she pays for the  infraction was something called hours  you might remember Errol Flynn's George  Custer marching back and forth with his  rifle as punishment for a misdemeanor at  the beginning of they died with their  boots on that's when Olivia de Havilland  walked by and distracts him but he's  supposed to be walking hours and cadets  can still be found marching back and  forth on the area as it's called and  they describe it as a mind-numbing  experience and I've put more than a few  of them there how was your weekend I  asked one of my students one Monday  morning well he said without resentment  I spent most of it walking hours because  I was late for your class last week and  thus one finds oneself connected  implicated in strange ways  with different facets of the cadet  experience one doesn't simply teach  English there's a convoluted cause chain  of causes and effects while a Stephen  supposedly composed his poetry while  walking to work  Stephens poetry might be forever linked  for a cadet to the experience of wearing  through the soles of his shoes on the  area in Andy's absence I read the paper  myself and it was an eloquent parody of  Thoreau's argument that reproduced its  twists and turns with a painstaking  fidelity in short Andy refused to  subject himself to professor Sam it's  arbitrary tyranny by writing t